{%- comment -%}
  The merge animation, then a pinned frame with the explanation scrolling past it.

  Split out of tonescan-landing so the page composes from real sections in a JSON
  template. Page-wide CSS and JS are rendered once by layout/tonescan.liquid, so this
  file carries markup only and can be reordered or removed without taking them with it.
{%- endcomment -%}

<div class="tonescan-landing tw:text-neutral-200">
  {%- comment -%} Screenshots live in Shopify Files (Content > Files). {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign ts_cdn = 'https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0634/3199/9736/files' -%}

  <div class="ts-dives">

    {%- comment -%}
      HERO deep-dive, RGB is the flagship feature, so it gets the full-width treatment:
      centred header, one large screenshot on a glow, then a two-column detail grid beneath.
      The other three rows below stay side-by-side, which keeps this one clearly on top.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <section class="ts-hdive" id="rgb">
      {%- comment -%}
        Scroll-linked channel merge: the three raw captures splay apart, draw together as you
        scroll, then the real converted frame crossfades in. Driven by --s / .is-converted in
        the script below. The organise-grid screenshot is the bento's RGB tile, so this shows
        the idea rather than repeating that.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Pinned region, same idea as the workflow story but with the media on the left.
        .ts-hdive__sticky holds the merged panel for the whole run; --t slides it left over
        the first screen, then it stays put while .ts-hdive__textcol scrolls past on the
        right. The text column is pulled up by one viewport so its first panel lines up with
        the image once the slide finishes.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="ts-hdive__pin">
        <div class="ts-hdive__sticky">
          <div class="ts-hdive__stage">
            {%- comment -%} One layer per channel so they can fade independently as the copy
               blocks go past. Three gradients on one element could not be animated apart. {%- endcomment -%}
            <div class="ts-hdive__glow" aria-hidden="true"><i class="g-r"></i><i class="g-g"></i><i class="g-b"></i></div>
            <div class="ts-rgb">
              <div class="ts-rgb__stage">
                <div class="ts-rgb__ch ts-rgb__ch--r"></div>
                <div class="ts-rgb__ch ts-rgb__ch--g"></div>
                <div class="ts-rgb__ch ts-rgb__ch--b"></div>
                <div class="ts-rgb__frame" data-i="0">
                  <div class="ts-rgb__result"></div>
                </div>

                {%- comment -%} panels 2 and 3 swap the frame's contents for a screenshot,
                   same frame footprint so nothing about the panel jumps {%- endcomment -%}
                <div class="ts-hdive__shot" data-i="1" aria-hidden="true">
                  <div class="ts-hdive__shotimg" style="--shot:url('{{ ts_cdn }}/calibration-crosstalk.png')"></div>
                </div>
                <div class="ts-hdive__shot" data-i="2" aria-hidden="true">
                  <div class="ts-hdive__shotimg" style="--shot:url('{{ ts_cdn }}/rgb-merge.png')"></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ts-hdive__rail">
          <div class="ts-hdive__dots">
            <button type="button" class="ts-hdive__dot is-active" data-i="0" aria-label="Go to: RGB scanning made simple"></button>
            <button type="button" class="ts-hdive__dot" data-i="1" aria-label="Go to: Why it beats white light"></button>
            <button type="button" class="ts-hdive__dot" data-i="2" aria-label="Go to: What you need to shoot it"></button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ts-hdive__textcol">
          <div class="ts-hdive__panel">
            <h2 class="ts-hdive__title ts-hdive__title--aside">RGB scanning made simple</h2>
            <p class="ts-hdive__sub ts-hdive__sub--aside">The way high-end lab scanners have always got their colour, now automatic on your own camera.</p>

            <h3 class="ts-hdive__ptitle ts-hdive__ptitle--sub">Three exposures, one frame</h3>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">The negative is photographed three times, under red, green and blue light in turn. Each exposure reads a single dye layer on its own.</p>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">You do not line anything up. toneScan Studio finds the three frames in your capture folder, matches them channel by channel and combines them.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="ts-hdive__panel">
            <h3 class="ts-hdive__ptitle">Why it beats white light</h3>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">Under white light your sensor sees all three dye layers at once, through its own colour filters, and the channels end up bleeding into each other.</p>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">Separating the exposures keeps each layer measured largely on its own, so there is far less crosstalk to unpick afterwards and far more room in the shadows before colour starts to fall apart.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="ts-hdive__panel">
            <h3 class="ts-hdive__ptitle">What you need to shoot it</h3>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">Any digital camera and a light source that can give you red, green and blue separately. Monochrome sensors work too.</p>
            <p class="ts-hdive__lead">A companion web app that turns your phone into the light source is on the way, so you will be able to try RGB with nothing more than the screen in your pocket.</p>
            <a class="ts-hdive__link" href="#signup">Try it in the beta <span aria-hidden="true">›</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ts-dive">
      <div class="ts-dive__inner">
        <div class="ts-dive__text">
          <p class="ts-dive__eyebrow">Your library</p>
          <h2 class="ts-dive__title">Every roll, in one place</h2>
          <p class="ts-dive__body">toneScan Studio keeps your scans organised by roll, the way you actually shoot. Point it at a folder and it watches for new captures, merges the channel triplets and files everything where you expect it. No separate cataloguing step.</p>
          <ul class="ts-bullets">
            <li>Rolls in a sidebar with live frame counts</li>
            <li>Watch folders that ingest new scans as they land</li>
            <li>Markers and colour labels for fast culling</li>
            <li>Sync one frame's settings across the whole roll</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ts-dive__media">
          <div class="ts-shot">
            <img src="{{ ts_cdn }}/organise.png" alt="The organise view with a list of rolls on the left and a grid of converted frames on the right" loading="lazy" width="3200" height="2000">
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ts-dive ts-dive--alt ts-dive--flip">
      <div class="ts-dive__inner">
        <div class="ts-dive__text">
          <p class="ts-dive__eyebrow">Calibration</p>
          <h2 class="ts-dive__title">Profiled to your own rig</h2>
          <p class="ts-dive__body">Calibration tools to dial in your scan exposure before you start, so each channel lands where it should. Save the result as a profile and reuse it across rolls rather than setting up from scratch every time.</p>
          <ul class="ts-bullets">
            <li>Per-channel EV meter showing exactly how far off you are</li>
            <li>Crosstalk measured from your own setup</li>
            <li>Save a profile and reuse it across rolls</li>
            <li>Automatic calibration when paired with toneLight</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ts-dive__media">
          <div class="ts-shot">
            <img src="{{ ts_cdn }}/calibration-crosstalk.png" alt="The calibrate screen measuring red, green and blue channels with the crosstalk matrix open" loading="lazy" width="3200" height="2000">
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {%- comment -%} Contact sheets, shipping in Studio. {%- endcomment -%}
    <section class="ts-dive">
      <div class="ts-dive__inner">
        <div class="ts-dive__text">
          <p class="ts-dive__eyebrow">Contact sheets</p>
          <h2 class="ts-dive__title">A whole roll on one sheet</h2>
          <p class="ts-dive__body">Lay a roll out as a proper 35mm contact sheet, the way it used to come back from the lab, and send it out as a JPG or a PDF. Frame numbers, edge printing and sprocket holes are all drawn to real film dimensions rather than pasted into a grid of thumbnails.</p>
          <ul class="ts-bullets">
            <li>A whole roll laid out as 35mm strips</li>
            <li>Frame numbers and edge printing along every strip</li>
            <li>Drawn to real film geometry, not a thumbnail grid</li>
            <li>Export as JPG or PDF</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ts-dive__media">
          <div class="ts-shot">
            {%- if section.settings.contact_sheet_image != blank -%}
            <img src="{{ section.settings.contact_sheet_image | image_url: width: 3200 }}" alt="A roll laid out as a 35mm contact sheet" loading="lazy" width="{{ section.settings.contact_sheet_image.width }}" height="{{ section.settings.contact_sheet_image.height }}">
            {%- else -%}
            <img src="{{ ts_cdn }}/organise.png" alt="" loading="lazy" width="3200" height="2000">
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "toneScan RGB deep dive",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "contact_sheet_image",
      "label": "Contact sheet screenshot",
      "info": "Falls back to a placeholder until a real one is uploaded."
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "toneScan RGB deep dive"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
